    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation",
    "problem": "The angle $\\theta =-{210}^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
    "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${150}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933898/assets/788edaa48306f8370f9780cdeda956a85c34b76bd6ec5a028d9d38d425cbc5b9.png)\n\nWe see that ${150}^{∘}$ lies in quadrant II. Therefore, $-{210}^{∘}$ also lies in this quadrant.",
